# SABOTAGE: D4 (T69) gate sabotage — defeats the MCP server's org-scope isolation (ADR-043
# §9: every tool is org-scoped; an MCP session for org A can never reach org B's data). The
# `browse` tool's records read (`Samen.AI.Mcp.read_records/4`) applies a HARD `org_id == ^org`
# filter — the "correct filter parameter" mechanism the embeddings plane and custom-object
# catalog use — so org B's browse never returns org A's rows. This sabotage drops that filter,
# so `browse` returns records across ALL orgs: an MCP session bound to org B now sees org A's
# records at the MCP boundary (the exact EG4 / org-isolation breach §9 forbids). `_ = org` keeps
# the arg bound so the tree still compiles; the isolation is simply gone. Dropping it flips the
# named cross-org browse test (org B must see zero of org A's records), while the same-org
# positive control and the masking tests stay green — a precise, refutable proof that org
# isolation is the load-bearing guard.
# APP: samen_core
# TEST_FILES: test/ai/mcp_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: (c) org-scope: org B's browse never returns org A's records
diff --git a/samen_core/lib/samen/ai/mcp.ex b/samen_core/lib/samen/ai/mcp.ex
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/samen_core/lib/samen/ai/mcp.ex
+++ b/samen_core/lib/samen/ai/mcp.ex
@@ -482,9 +482,15 @@
   # ==========================================================================
 
   defp read_records(org, resource, lim, opts) do
+    # SABOTAGE (T69 §9 org-scope bypass): the MCP browse read drops the hard `org_id` filter,
+    # so `browse` returns records across ALL orgs — an MCP session bound to org B now sees
+    # org A's records (the EG4 / org-isolation breach ADR-043 §9 forbids). `_ = org` keeps the
+    # arg bound so the tree still compiles; the isolation is simply gone. Flips the named
+    # cross-org browse test.
+    _ = org
+
     query =
       resource
-      |> Ash.Query.filter(org_id == ^org)
       |> Ash.Query.limit(lim)
       |> ensure_pii_selected(resource)
 
